{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghx6hQuVAsPjo1q5peWx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I closed the gaps you listed, and I was wrong to say the goal's conditions held before. The game now has the practice range, both dev labs, and a local party mode. All checks pass, and the AI Hub harness passes on the final bundle. Two things can't be verified from this machine: a real gamepad and a real touch device.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Practice range:** four stations, each with its own targets and goal. Paper targets, crows, a pump drill, and a long-range buck. Hitting the goal ends a station early. Nothing is saved.\n- **Animal Lab** (Dev mode): every animal, its walk, graze, bolt, stare and fall states, and the hit-zone outlines.\n- **Sound Lab** (Dev mode): every sound effect, song, and cast voice, playable on demand.\n- **Local multiplayer:** a hot-seat party hunt for 2 to 4 players. Each site is played by every player in turn on the same seed, so the animals match, and the highest total wins. It is not simultaneous split-screen. TASK.md doesn't require local play; I added this because PLAN.md listed it.\n- **Attract mode** also starts after 10 s idle on the top menu, as well as on the title.\n\n**Checks:** `node tests/run.mjs` runs 189 checks. They cover the whole-campaign playthrough, the range, party turn order, both labs, the hub round, and a Chromium pass over every screen. The AI Hub harness passes at 2 players and at 4 players with latency, jitter and loss.\n\n**Not verified:**\n- Real gamepad input. The button mapping follows the standard layout, but I had no controller.\n- Real touch devices. The touch-only screen was tested only with a stubbed device check.\n\n**Deliverables:** `BigBuckHunterHoedown-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-054528.html` and `exit-interview.json` in the work root. The feedback answers are updated to match.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:45:28\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade light-gun hunting shooter with a trophy encounter, bonus games, a practice range, and a hot-seat party mode. First person on rails, over a pseudo-3D landscape, with an x-ray Shot Chart and 2D menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine (unmodified), AI Hub client lib/hub session.js 1.4.0 (verbatim), vanilla JavaScript, Node 24, Playwright with Chromium\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's retro-modern pixel style. Landscapes, animals and paper targets are painted in code.\n- **Animation:** Procedural in code (gait, grazing, bolting, staring, falling). The engine's rigs were not used.\n- **Audio:** Bluegrass chiptune songs, gunshots, bugle and bird calls, and Animalese voices, all synthesized\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs` (189 checks, including a scripted campaign and a Chromium pass); the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players under stress. Gamepad and touch hardware not tested.\n- **Research:** Planning-stage reading of the Big Buck Hunter and Big Buck Hunter Pro pages (PLAN.md §1.1)\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser for the player unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About 9 game issues in the first build, and a few more while adding the new modes. The new-mode bugs were a range station crash on start and a test-timing mismatch.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** 1 critical in the first build: the results screen crashed after the last segment. It's fixed and covered by the browser pass.\n- **Most helpful test:** The Chromium pass over every screen, which caught the crash and the visual faults.\n- **Least helpful test:** The same-seed determinism check, which only confirms the seeded streams repeat.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The overlay and pixel primitives made a custom scene easy. The standard menus came free.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Scene changes fade, so keys sent right after one hit the old scene. A scripted tap marks the device as \"touch\", which makes `input.label()` show action names. The font has no middle dot or en dash, so they print as \"?\".\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** The hunt: landscape, animals, hit zones, the pump model, the bot, the Shot Chart, the range, the party mode, and the labs.\n- **Hardest part:** Fitting an on-rails, screen-space shooter into an engine built around camera views and lit scenes.\n- **Engine changes:** None. The engine file is the handoff copy, unmodified.\n- **One change I'd make:** Make `label()` fall back to keyboard names after a scripted tap, and cover the middle dot and en dash in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The explicit core loop, the exact deliverable names, and the testing rules.\n- **Confusing:** PLAN.md names a different title and dev folder from TASK.md, and describes far more than one session's worth of work. It also doesn't say which parts are required. The gap between \"feature-complete\" and \"every feature in the plan\" made it unclear when to stop.\n- **Hardest:** Finishing the plan's systems in one session, and answering the usage and feedback questions honestly without measurements.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark which plan features are required and which are optional. Say whether local multiplayer must be simultaneous. Give a scope budget for one session."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1150,"cache_read_input_tokens":372733,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1150},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sessi