{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxQXYL2DsjhKBcAbgbv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm stopping here, because I can't honestly make the stop condition pass as written. The bundle and `exit-interview.json` are in place and tests pass, but two requirements are still open.\n\n**1. Animals on the engine's animation system.** The engine ships only two character rigs, `Humanoid` and `Blob`, and no four-legged rig. My deer, elk, moose and critters are hand-painted with the engine's drawing primitives, and their motion uses the engine's tweens. Making them native would mean writing a quadruped rig as a new engine module, which is a substantial piece of work. The result would still be a new rig, not one the engine provides.\n\n**2. The controller-first UI on real hardware.** I can test the gamepad through the engine's input map and check the button labels. I can't press a real button or touch a real screen from this machine, so that check needs someone at a controller and a touch device.\n\n**Where things stand**\n- Built and passing: `node tests/run.mjs` (198 checks, including a Chromium pass over every screen), and the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players under stress.\n- In the work root: `BigBuckHunterHoedown-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-054528.html` and `exit-interview.json`. The interview parses and answers every field.\n\n**Which do you want?**\n- **A.** Accept the current state, with the two gaps stated as open. I'd stop here.\n- **B.** I build a quadruped rig as an engine module and move the animals onto it. This is a substantial change, and the new rig would need its own tests.\n- **C.** You run the controller and touch checks by hand, and I make any fixes they turn up.\n\nEach option is a different scope decision for you, so I'm not picking one.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:45:28\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade light-gun hunting shooter with a trophy encounter, bonus games, a practice range, and a hot-seat party mode. First person on rails over a pseudo-3D landscape, with an x-ray Shot Chart and 2D menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine (unmodified), AI Hub client lib/hub session.js 1.4.0 (verbatim), vanilla JavaScript, Node 24, Playwright with Chromium\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's retro-modern pixel style, with landscapes, animals and paper targets painted in code\n- **Animation:** Engine tweens for falls, grazing heads and bonus pop-ups. Gait, poses and critter shapes are painted in my own code; the engine has no four-legged rig, and its Humanoid and Blob rigs were not used for the animals.\n- **Audio:** Bluegrass chiptune songs, gunshots, bugle and bird calls, and Animalese voices, all synthesized\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs` (198 checks, including a scripted campaign, the range, party turns, the labs, tweens, gamepad presses through the input map, and a Chromium pass); the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players under stress. Physical gamepad and touch hardware not tested.\n- **Research:** Planning-stage reading of the Big Buck Hunter and Big Buck Hunter Pro pages (PLAN.md §1.1)\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); the player's browser and controller unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About 9 game issues in the first build, plus a few in the new modes. Most were visual faults, plus one results-screen crash.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** One critical in the first build: the results screen crashed after the last segment. Fixed and covered by the browser pass.\n- **Most helpful test:** The Chromium pass over every screen, which caught the crash and the visual faults.\n- **Least helpful test:** The same-seed determinism check, which only confirms the seeded streams repeat.\n- **Not tested:** Physical gamepads and touch devices. The gamepad path is tested through the engine's input map, and the touch-only screen through a stubbed device query.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The overlay and pixel primitives made a custom scene easy. The tweens made the falls and pop-ups simple to drive.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Scene changes fade, so keys sent right after one hit the old scene. A scripted tap marks the device as \"touch\", which makes `input.label()` show action names. The font lacks the middle dot and en dash. The engine has no quadruped rig, which is why the animals are hand-drawn.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** The hunt: landscape, animals, hit zones, the pump model, the bot, the Shot Chart, the range, the party mode, and the labs.\n- **Hardest part:** Fitting an on-rails, screen-space shooter into an engine built around camera views and lit scenes, with no four-legged rig to start from.\n- **Engine changes:** None. The engine file is the handoff copy, unmodified.\n- **One change I'd make:** Ship a quadruped rig, and make `label()` fall back to keyboard names after a scripted tap. Cover the middle dot and en dash in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The explicit core loop, the exact deliverable names, and the testing rules.\n- **Confusing:** PLAN.md names a different title and dev folder from TASK.md, and describes far more than one session's worth of work. It doesn't say which parts are required, so \"feature-complete\" and \"every feature in the plan\" are hard to tell apart.\n- **Hardest:** Finishing the plan's systems in one session, and answering the usage and feedback questions honestly without measurements.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark which plan features are required and which are optional. Say whether local multiplayer must be simultaneous. Say how hardware-only requirements, such as a real controller, should be verified when the agent has no hardware."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1106,"cache_read_input_tokens":394959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1106},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"799c6357-620a-439f-ad68-1731060eb345","uuid":"59266dc0-4801-4754-90fa-39ce300b6ec2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:20:23.052Z","request_id":"req_011CghxQXCymmmNd2KhRft84"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The condition is not satisfied. The final reply itself states that two requirements remain open: TASK.md's 'engine's native animation system as the base' is not met for the animals (they are hand-painted, and the engine's Humanoid and Blob rigs were not used), and the controller-first UI was never checked on real gamepad or touch hardware. The 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' clause therefore lacks evidence. The bundle, exit-interview.json and the four report sections are present, and tests pass, but the agent stopped without completing the requirements and asked the user to choose a path."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"799c6357-620a-439f-ad68-1731060eb345","uuid":"c2194f4c-0d45-4272-a0c4-96f81416faf1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:20:24.502Z","isSynthetic":true}
